No search input in this dir (config dir, no app code) → generic version. Vanilla:

```js
const input = document.querySelector('#search');
let timer, controller;

input.addEventListener('input', () => {
  clearTimeout(timer);
  timer = setTimeout(async () => {
    controller?.abort();                 // cancel in-flight request
    controller = new AbortController();
    const q = input.value.trim();
    if (!q) return render([]);
    try {
      const res = await fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(q)}`, { signal: controller.signal });
      render(await res.json());
    } catch (e) {
      if (e.name !== 'AbortError') throw e;
    }
  }, 300);
});
```

React:

```jsx
function Search() {
  const [q, setQ] = useState('');
  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!q.trim()) return setResults([]);
    const controller = new AbortController();
    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(q)}`, { signal: controller.signal })
        .then(r => r.json())
        .then(setResults)
        .catch(e => { if (e.name !== 'AbortError') throw e; });
    }, 300);
    return () => { clearTimeout(timer); controller.abort(); };
  }, [q]);

  return <input value={q} onChange={e => setQ(e.target.value)} />;
}
```

`/api/search` and `render` are placeholders for your endpoint and results renderer. Untested.

Debounce alone doesn't stop a slow early response landing after a later one → stale results; the `AbortController` covers that. 300ms is the usual starting delay.

